</history><permissions><!--&#x000a9; 2024 John Wiley & Sons Ltd and Medicalhelplines.com Inc.--><copyright-statement content-type="article-copyright">&#x000a9; 2023 The Authors. <italic toggle="yes">International Wound Journal</italic> published by Medicalhelplines.com Inc and John Wiley &#x00026; Sons Ltd.</copyright-statement><license><ali:license_ref xmlns:ali="http://www.niso.org/schemas/ali/1.0/" specific-use="textmining" content-type="ccbynclicense">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/</ali:license_ref><license-p>This is an open access article under the terms of the <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/">http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/</ext-link> License, which permits use, distribution and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited and is not used for commercial purposes.</license-p></license></permissions><self-uri content-type="pdf" xlink:href="file:IWJ-21-e14443.pdf"/><abstract><title>Abstract</title><p>Prostate cancer is one of the most common malignancies worldwide and the fifth leading cause of cancer deaths in men. With the rapidly increasing surgical rate of minimally invasive radical prostatectomy, there is still controversy about how to use a urinary catheter post&#x02010;operatively. Thus, we attempted to compare the post&#x02010;operative wound&#x02010;related outcomes through a meta&#x02010;analysis of urethral catheterisation (UC) versus suprapubic catheterisation (SPC) after minimally invasive radical prostatectomy. As of August 2023, the authors conducted systematic searches in databases such as PubMed, Embase, Web of Science and the Cochrane Library. The authors reviewed the relevant literature separately to determine comparisons between SPC and UC treatment after radical prostatectomy. A total of 395 subjects were enrolled in the five trials, met the eligibility criteria and were included in the meta&#x02010;analysis. Data collection and analysis revealed significant differences in catheter bother to patients for surgical trauma (MD, 0.98; 95% CI, 0.48, 1.48 <italic toggle="no">p</italic>&#x02009;=&#x02009;0.0001), with SPC causing less catheter bother to patients post&#x02010;operatively; post&#x02010;operative catheter&#x02010;related problems (OR, 3.3; 95% CI, 0.03, 326.1 <italic toggle="no">p</italic>&#x02009;=&#x02009;0.61), the POD1 of the post&#x02010;operative period (MD, &#x02212; 0.09; 95% CI, &#x02212;0.75, 0.94 <italic toggle="no">p</italic>&#x02009;=&#x02009;0.83) and the POD3 of the post&#x02010;operative period (MD, &#x02212;0.49; 95% CI, &#x02212;0.99, 0.01 <italic toggle="no">p</italic>&#x02009;=&#x02009;0.06); there was no statistically significant difference in wound pain. Compared with UC, SPC patients had less post&#x02010;operative catheter distress. Thus, SPC is more beneficial in reducing post&#x02010;operative wound discomfort in patients. The validity of the results remains to be tested in more and better studies.</p></abstract><kwd-group kwd-group-type="author-generated"><kwd id="iwj14443-kwd-0001">radical prostatectomy</kwd><kwd id="iwj14443-kwd-0002">suprapubic catheter</kwd><kwd id="iwj14443-kwd-0003">urinary catheterisation</kwd><kwd id="iwj14443-kwd-0004">wound pain</kwd></kwd-group><counts><fig-count count="7"/><table-count count="2"/><page-count count="7"/><word-count count="3133"/></counts><custom-meta-group><custom-meta><meta-name>source-schema-version-number</meta-name><meta-value>2.0</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>cover-date</meta-name><meta-value>February 2024</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>details-of-publishers-convertor</meta-name><meta-value>Converter:WILEY_ML3GV2_TO_JATSPMC version:6.3.6 mode:remove_FC converted:31.01.2024</meta-value></custom-meta></custom-meta-group></article-meta><notes><p content-type="self-citation">
<mixed-citation publication-type="journal" id="iwj14443-cit-9001">
<string-name>
<surname>Wang</surname>
<given-names>T</given-names>
</string-name>, <string-name>
<surname>Ji</surname>
<given-names>S</given-names>
</string-name>, <string-name>
<surname>Zhang</surname>
<given-names>C</given-names>
</string-name>, <string-name>
<surname>Xiang</surname>
<given-names>Y</given-names>
</string-name>, <string-name>
<surname>Yin</surname>
<given-names>G</given-names>
</string-name>. <article-title>Effect of different catheter pathways on wounds after minimally invasive radical prostatectomy: A meta&#x02010;analysis</article-title>. <source>Int Wound J</source>. <year>2024</year>;<volume>21</volume>(<issue>2</issue>):<elocation-id>e14443</elocation-id>. doi:<pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1111/iwj.14443</pub-id>
</mixed-citation>
</p><fn-group id="iwj14443-ntgp-0001"><fn id="iwj14443-note-0001" fn-type="equal"><p>Tao Wang and ShiBen Ji have contributed equally to this work and share first authorship.</p></fn></fn-group></notes></front><body id="iwj14443-body-0001"><sec id="iwj14443-sec-0003"><label>1</label><title>INTRODUCTION</title><p>Prostate cancer is the second most frequently diagnosed malignant tumour worldwide and the fifth leading cause of cancer deaths in men.<xref rid="iwj14443-bib-0001" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>1</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="iwj14443-bib-0002" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>2</sup>
</xref> Radical prostatectomy is one of the most effective treatments for the treatment of low&#x02010; and intermediate&#x02010;risk prostate cancer. Because of the rise in clinical limitations and the use of minimally invasive surgery, there is a rapid rise in the rate of radical prostatectomy (RP) operations worldwide.<xref rid="iwj14443-bib-0003" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>3</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="iwj14443-bib-0004" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>4</sup>
</xref> RP is regarded as a gold standard in the management of prostate cancer.<xref rid="iwj14443-bib-0005" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>5</sup>
</xref> In recent years, RARP has become increasingly prominent globally.<xref rid="iwj14443-bib-0006" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>6</sup>
</xref> The need for robotic&#x02010;assisted radical prostatectomy (RARP) is growing, as it provides a clearer definition of the surgical anatomy and greater operability.<xref rid="iwj14443-bib-0007" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>7</sup>
</xref> RARP has the potential to improve the outcome of perioperatively, post&#x02010;operative complications and long duration of urinary incontinence.<xref rid="iwj14443-bib-0008" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>8</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="iwj14443-bib-0009" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>9</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>The question of how to administer urine catheter following RARP is, however, still a matter of controversy. The two most common RARP catheter pathways are the suprapubic region and the urethra. Urethral catheterisation (UC) is a conventional way to drain the bladder to avoid anastomosis stricture.<xref rid="iwj14443-bib-0010" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>10</sup>
</xref> During surgery, UC is still a major source of pain and discomfort.<xref rid="iwj14443-bib-0011" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>11</sup>
</xref> For the reduction in post&#x02010;operative pain, suprapubic catheterisation (SPC) has been applied in cardiac and abdomen operations, indicating that SPC is more effective than UCC in the post&#x02010;operative period.<xref rid="iwj14443-bib-0012" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>12</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="iwj14443-bib-0013" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>13</sup>
</xref> A number of studies have analysed the problems associated with the urethra, especially the penis, as one of the most frequent postsurgical complaints of patients.<xref rid="iwj14443-bib-0011" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>11</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="iwj14443-bib-0014" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>14</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="iwj14443-bib-0015" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>15</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="iwj14443-bib-0016" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>16</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="iwj14443-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>17</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="iwj14443-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>18</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="iwj14443-bib-0019" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>19</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>A number of initial studies indicate that post&#x02010;RARP drainage can be replaced by UC in a post&#x02010;RARP disposition.<xref rid="iwj14443-bib-0020" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>20</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="iwj14443-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>21</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="iwj14443-bib-0022" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>22</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="iwj14443-bib-0023" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>23</sup>
</xref> It has been demonstrated to provide bladder drainage equivalent to that of UC and can decrease penis pain and discomfort. Furthermore, SPC is linked to a reduced incidence of urethral trauma, urogenital infections and other complications.<xref rid="iwj14443-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>24</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="iwj14443-bib-0025" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>25</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="iwj14443-bib-0026" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>26</sup>
</xref> SPC was extensively applied at the beginning of the 21 st century, with the introduction of customized SPC following RARP.<xref rid="iwj14443-bib-0023" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>23</sup>
</xref> It is still not clear, however, if SPC is better than UC during RARP. This research is intended to provide a systematic review and meta&#x02010;analysis to evaluate SPC efficacy vs UC treatment following RARP.</p></sec><sec sec-type="methods" id="iwj14443-sec-0004"><label>2</label><title>METHODS</title><sec id="iwj14443-sec-0005"><label>2.1</label><title>Search strategy</title><p>In June 2023, a literature search was carried out on databases like PubMed, Embase and Cochrane Library. Language was limited to literature published in English. Keywords such as laparoscopy, microtrauma, robotic and RALP were found. The concrete search pattern policy is illustrated in Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="iwj14443-tbl-0001" ref-type="table">1</xref>. The search procedure is also done manually to find links to all related articles, and this is done in addition to a computerized search. Where more than one report has been released covering the same study, the latest or full report has been used.</p><table-wrap position="float" id="iwj14443-tbl-0001" content-type="TABLE"><label>TABLE 1</label><caption><p>Search strategy.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><thead valign="bottom"><tr style="border-bottom:solid 1px #000000"><th align="left" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">No.</th><th align="left" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Query</th></tr></thead><tbody valign="top"><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">#1</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Prostatic[Title/Abstract] OR Prostate[Title/Abstract] OR Prostatectomy[Title/Abstract]</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">#2</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Laparoscop*[Title/Abstract] OR Minimally invasive[Title/Abstract] OR robot*[Title/Abstract] OR RALP[Title/Abstract] OR RARP[Title/Abstract] OR Endoscopic extraperitoneal[Title/Abstract] OR LRP[Title/Abstract] OR EERPE[Title/Abstract] OR Telerobotics[Title/Abstract] OR Comput*[Title/Abstract] OR Remote operation*[Title/Abstract]</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">#3</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Suprapubic[Title/Abstract]</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">#4</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Urethral[Title/Abstract] OR Transurethral[Title/Abstract]</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">#5</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Pain*[All Files] OR Incision*[All Files] OR Infection[All Files] OR Dehiscence[All Files] OR Haemorrhage[All Files] OR Bleed*[All Files] OR Haematoma[All Files] OR Wound[All Files] OR Complication*[All Files]</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">#6</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">#1 AND #2 AND #3 AND #4 AND #5</td></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap></sec><sec id="iwj14443-sec-0006"><label>2.2</label><title>Inclusion and exclusion criteria</title><p>
<italic toggle="yes">Eligibility criteria</italic>: (1) Comparison of UC with SPC in prostate cancer. (2) Studies restricted to humans and sex (men). (3) Minimally invasive prostatic carcinoma therapy, including robotic or laparoscopy.</p><p>
<italic toggle="yes">Criteria for exclusion</italic>: (1) Conference summaries, case reports, correspondence or comments were not included in the analysis. (2) No comparison was made between UC and SPC catheterization on injury after surgery. The selection process is shown in Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="iwj14443-fig-0001" ref-type="fig">1</xref>.</p><fig position="float" fig-type="FIGURE" id="iwj14443-fig-0001"><label>FIGURE 1</label><caption><p>Flow chart of the study.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IWJ-21-e14443-g001" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-1"/></fig></sec><sec id="iwj14443-sec-0007"><label>2.3</label><title>Data extraction</title><p>Two authors have independently examined the headings, summaries and summaries of the research. If there is any disagreement, a third author is asked to take the final decision. Data were taken from eligible trials. The data obtained from these studies were as follows: post&#x02010;operative complications, including post&#x02010;operative pain (first and third day after operation). Patients were evaluated with VAS or NRS after surgery (Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="iwj14443-tbl-0002" ref-type="table">2</xref>).</p><table-wrap position="float" id="iwj14443-tbl-0002" content-type="TABLE"><label>TABLE 2</label><caption><p>Distribution characteristics of the selected studies used for meta&#x02010;analysis.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><thead valign="bottom"><tr style="border-bottom:solid 1px #000000"><th align="left" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Study</th><th align="left" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Country</th><th align="left" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Year</th><th align="left" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Uc</th><th align="left" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Age</th><th align="left" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Spc</th><th align="left" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Age</th></tr></thead><tbody valign="top"><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Afzal<xref rid="iwj14443-bib-0027" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>27</sup>
</xref>
</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">America</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2015</td><td align="char" char="." valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">174</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">63.7&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;7.0</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">51</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">61.9&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;6.9</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Martinschek<xref rid="iwj14443-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>18</sup>
</xref>
</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Germany</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2016</td><td align="char" char="." valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">35</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02014;</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">27</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02014;</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Prasad<xref rid="iwj14443-bib-0028" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>28</sup>
</xref>
</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">America</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2014</td><td align="char" char="." valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">29</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">57.7&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;8.6</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">29</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">60.0&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;6.4</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Tewari<xref rid="iwj14443-bib-0023" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>23</sup>
</xref>
</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">America</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2008</td><td align="char" char="." valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">20</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">60.0 (55.8&#x02014;66.3)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">10</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">60.8 (52.8&#x02013;67.3)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Yang<xref rid="iwj14443-bib-0019" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>19</sup>
</xref>
</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">China</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2015</td><td align="char" char="." valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">10</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">68.5 (61&#x02013;74)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">10</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">64.6 (49&#x02013;74)</td></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap></sec><sec id="iwj14443-sec-0008"><label>2.4</label><title>Quality assessment</title><p>Documentation quality evaluation procedures were performed with ROBINS&#x02010;I, a hypothetical &#x0201c;Goal Test&#x0201d;, which did not require realism or ethics to achieve the desired outcome of a desirable research. Every assessed study is considered an approximate result of the objective test, and the responses to the &#x0201c;Signalling Questions&#x0201d; are then used to decide if a dimension is classified as &#x0201c;Low, Medium, Severe&#x0201d;. Depending on the degree of hazard in every aspect, the paper makes use of the standard of hazard evaluation to decide the degree of risk. Quality evaluation results are shown in Figures&#x000a0;<xref rid="iwj14443-fig-0002" ref-type="fig">2</xref> and <xref rid="iwj14443-fig-0003" ref-type="fig">3</xref>.</p><fig position="float" fig-type="FIGURE" id="iwj14443-fig-0002"><label>FIGURE 2</label><caption><p>Risk of bias diagram.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IWJ-21-e14443-g004" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-3"/></fig><fig position="float" fig-type="FIGURE" id="iwj14443-fig-0003"><label>FIGURE 3</label><caption><p>Summary of bias diagram.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IWJ-21-e14443-g006" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-5"/></fig></sec><sec id="iwj14443-sec-0009"><label>2.5</label><title>Data analysis</title><p>The meta&#x02010;analysis was conducted with RevMan 5.3. The comparison of consecutive and binary variables was carried out by average difference (MD) and odds ratio (OR). A 95% confidence interval was reported for all outcomes. Average and standard deviations have been estimated for the studies that include intermediate, interquartile intervals or ranges. The chi&#x02010;square method was applied to examine the statistical heterogeneity among the trials, and the <italic toggle="yes">I</italic>
<sup>2</sup> statistic was used to quantify the heterogeneity. A random&#x02010;effects model is applied if there is considerable heterogeneity among the trials; on the contrary, a fixed&#x02010;effects model is applied.</p></sec></sec><sec sec-type="results" id="iwj14443-sec-0010"><label>3</label><title>RESULTS</title><p>Data collection and analysis showed that SPC caused less catheter distress to patients post&#x02010;operatively compared with UC (MD, 0.98; 95% CI, 0.48, 1.48 <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x02009;=&#x02009;0.0001). No statistical significance was found for the rate of catheter&#x02010;related problems (OR, 3.3; 95% CI, 0.03, 326.1 <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x02009;=&#x02009;0.61). After the operation, there were no statistically significant differences in wound pain at the first day (MD, &#x02212;0.09; 95% CI, &#x02212;0.75, 0.94 <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x02009;=&#x02009;0.83) and day three (MD, &#x02212;0.49; 95% CI, &#x02212;0.99, 0.01 <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x02009;=&#x02009;0.06). There was also no significant difference in terms of pain scores, Figures&#x000a0;<xref rid="iwj14443-fig-0004" ref-type="fig">4</xref>, <xref rid="iwj14443-fig-0005" ref-type="fig">5</xref>, <xref rid="iwj14443-fig-0006" ref-type="fig">6</xref>, <xref rid="iwj14443-fig-0007" ref-type="fig">7</xref>.</p><fig position="float" fig-type="FIGURE" id="iwj14443-fig-0004"><label>FIGURE 4</label><caption><p>Forest plot of catheter bother in UC versus SPC after minimally invasive radical prostatectomy.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IWJ-21-e14443-g003" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-7"/></fig><fig position="float" fig-type="FIGURE" id="iwj14443-fig-0005"><label>FIGURE 5</label><caption><p>Forest plot of catheter&#x02010;related problems in UC versus SPC after minimally invasive radical prostatectomy.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IWJ-21-e14443-g002" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-9"/></fig><fig position="float" fig-type="FIGURE" id="iwj14443-fig-0006"><label>FIGURE 6</label><caption><p>Forest of effects plot for post&#x02010;operative UC compared to SPC post&#x02010;operative day one wound pain in subjects undergoing minimally invasive prostate cancer surgery.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IWJ-21-e14443-g007" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-11"/></fig><fig position="float" fig-type="FIGURE" id="iwj14443-fig-0007"><label>FIGURE 7</label><caption><p>Forest of effects plot for wound pain on day three post&#x02010;operative UC compared to SPC in subjects undergoing minimally invasive prostate cancer surgery.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IWJ-21-e14443-g005" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-13"/></fig></sec><sec sec-type="discussion" id="iwj14443-sec-0011"><label>4</label><title>DISCUSSION</title><p>While research has been done on the efficacy of both channels, the primary documents contained in the preceding research are premature, few and poor in quality, and there has not been a review of the comparison of urinary catheter patterns following RARP. Recently, with the development of microinvasive surgical techniques, the application of robotics has become more and more popular, especially RARP, as a kind of novel treatment for prostate cancer. More recently, a new study has shown that the use of suprapubic catheter is beneficial for the treatment of asymptomatic bacterial infections, as well as pain associated with urethral catheterization.<xref rid="iwj14443-bib-0025" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>25</sup>
</xref> This trial, however, involved a bigger group and compared the effectiveness of the two types of catheter placement in the in&#x02010;hospital setting. We also restricted the trial to a subset of patients who received minimal invasive procedures for prostate cancer. As far as we know, UC is a traditional treatment for RP, not only for draining the urine, but also for preserving anastomoses and facilitating healing. In recent years, some researches have tried to use SPC in place of UC for the improvement of prostate cancer in the treatment of robotic RARP. These findings are contradictory, and we have systematically sought out and gathered comparisons between UC and SPC following RARP and have performed meta&#x02010;analyses on this subject to identify which kind of catheter can decrease the post&#x02010;operative pain. Thus, it is our view that systematic aggregation and consolidation of knowledge in this field is necessary to guide clinical decision making and practice and to improve patient selection.</p><p>The main endpoint of the trial was post&#x02010;operative pain, which consisted of the 1st and 3rd days after surgery as well as the urinary tract complications associated with the surrounding injury. In all initial trials, the number of patients and their pain scores were documented by means of a pain rating system. And subpopulations were analysed at 1 and 3&#x02009;days after surgery. In this meta&#x02010;analysis, we chose 5 publications out of 318 related trials that were reviewed for inclusion and released from 2008 to 2016. Among 395 cases who received radical prostatectomy, 268 were treated with UC and 127 were treated with SPC. The overall population was between 10 and 174.</p><p>We have some advantages for this research. All the research we have done up to now, for instance, has been published over the past several years, and we have assessed the literature quality of the included studies. Those are the merits of the present research. However, it also has certain limitations, because the majority of the research is a cohort study with an average quality, which might affect the outcome. Furthermore, there are few trials currently available, so it is necessary to carry out many high&#x02010;quality trials at a later date in order to validate their findings.</p></sec><sec sec-type="conclusions" id="iwj14443-sec-0012"><label>5</label><title>CONCLUSION</title><p>Patients who received SPC post&#x02010;operatively had less catheter bother due to post&#x02010;operative wounds compared with those who received UC. There was no statistically significant difference in post&#x02010;operative pain or catheter&#x02010;related injuries. Thus, SPC causes less catheter distress to patients post&#x02010;operatively compared with UC. The validity of these findings needs to be further explored with more and better studies.</p></sec><sec sec-type="COI-statement" id="iwj14443-sec-0014"><title>CONFLICT OF INTEREST STATEMENT</title><p>The authors declare no conflicts of interest.</p></sec></body><back><ack id="iwj14443-sec-0013"><title>ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS</title><p>We thank Prof. Guohong Yin for his review of this study and suggestions for revisions.</p></ack><sec sec-type="data-availability" id="iwj14443-sec-0016"><title>DATA AVAILABILITY STATEMENT</title><p>Data available on request from the authors.</p></sec><ref-list id="iwj14443-bibl-0001" content-type="cited-references"><title>REFERENCES</title><ref id="iwj14443-bib-0001"><label>1</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal" id="iwj14443-cit-0001">
